VALUES ('64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'module-01-compute-practice', 'Module 1 (Practice Scenarios) - Deploy and Manage Azure Compute Resources',
        NULL, 7);

VALUES ('b2a03ca1-4512-4b50-97f9-9237a75bbb8c', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso is planning a lift-and-shift migration of an on-premises SQL Server database to an Azure VM. The database requires very high disk throughput and consistent low-latency IOPS, and the workload runs 24/7 with no tolerance for downtime during business hours. Which combination best satisfies the disk and pricing requirements while minimizing unnecessary spend?',
        'Storage-optimized VMs are designed for high disk throughput and IOPS, ideal for database workloads, and pairing them with Premium SSD or Ultra Disk delivers the low-latency, high-IOPS performance SQL Server needs. Since the workload runs continuously and predictably, a Reserved VM Instance (1 or 3 year commitment) offers up to 72% savings versus pay-as-you-go. Option A''s Standard HDD offers far lower IOPS and throughput, unsuitable for a production database. Option C''s GPU VM is designed for graphics/ML workloads, not database I/O, and there is no "Free tier" for standard VM sizes. Option D pairs the right pricing model with the wrong VM category (compute-optimized targets CPU-heavy workloads, not disk I/O) and the wrong disk type.', 1, TRUE);

VALUES ('ae536342-7e30-43bc-a1b9-55ad34338560', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'An administrator at Contoso is resizing a running production VM to a larger size to handle increased load, but the Azure portal reports that the target size is not available. The administrator does not want to accept any downtime risk of losing the current temp disk contents unnecessarily. What is the most likely cause and the recommended next step?',
        'A running VM can only be resized to sizes available on its current hardware cluster; the portal only displays valid options. Stopping and deallocating the VM removes it from that cluster, making any size available in the region selectable — at the cost of losing the temporary disk''s contents and triggering a brief restart, which is an acceptable trade-off outside business-critical windows. Option B is false; resizing without deletion is routinely possible when the size is available. Option C describes a subscription quota issue, which is unrelated to size availability errors. Option D is not accurate — resizing does not require a region migration.', 2, TRUE);

VALUES ('75f3d196-7aed-4152-8d55-cb6a2d9086ba', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s finance team wants to minimize compute costs for a batch-processing VM that only needs to run for about 2 hours each night. They ask whether they will be billed for the full 24-hour day even though the VM is idle most of the time. What is the accurate billing behavior, assuming the VM is properly stopped and deallocated after each nightly run?',
        'Compute is priced hourly but billed by the minute, and no compute charge applies once the VM is stopped and deallocated, since deallocation releases the underlying hardware. However, storage used by the VM''s disks is billed independently of the VM''s running state, so storage costs continue even during the idle hours. Option C is incorrect, since Azure explicitly bills only for actual usage minutes. Option A is wrong because simply "stopping" a VM (without deallocating) does not stop compute billing — full deallocation is required, and even then storage keeps billing. Option D describes a threshold that does not exist in Azure''s billing model.', 3, TRUE);

VALUES ('61a3515e-59ee-4ba1-8a26-34586462ab93', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso needs to deploy 40 identical VMs across three environments (dev, test, and production) with only the VM name, network name, and storage account name varying between environments. The solution must be reusable, source-controllable, and repeatable without manual portal clicks. Which approach best satisfies these requirements?',
        'ARM templates are JSON files that define the resources to deploy and can be parameterized for fields like VM name, network name, and storage account name, then redeployed repeatedly with different parameter values per environment — satisfying reusability, source control, and repeatability. Option B is exactly the labor-intensive, error-prone, and non-repeatable process the team wants to avoid. Option D is incorrect because VM extensions configure post-deployment software tasks on an existing VM, not the identity of network or storage resources at creation time. Option A is unrelated; Auto-shutdown only schedules automatic VM shutdowns and has no role in replicating deployments.', 4, TRUE);

VALUES ('930021c1-200b-46f0-9cf6-b4cef675797d', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s security team requires that a critical application''s data and infrastructure survive the complete loss of an entire Azure datacenter, with automatic, near-instant availability of the application and data in another physically separate location within the same region. Which Azure capability, described in the module, most directly satisfies this specific requirement?',
        'Availability zones are physically separate locations within a region, each with independent power, cooling, and networking. Designing solutions to use replicated VMs across zones protects applications and data from datacenter-level failures, since if one zone is compromised, replicated resources are instantly available in another zone. Option D is unrelated to resiliency; extensions handle post-deployment configuration tasks. Option A only controls scheduled shutdowns to save cost and has nothing to do with fault tolerance. Option C is a billing/cost optimization choice with no bearing on datacenter-failure resiliency.', 5, TRUE);

VALUES ('5040b0a4-c8a2-4be2-97dc-557f4973f5fa', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'A Contoso administrator exported an ARM template from an existing production VM to use as a baseline for new deployments but noticed the VM''s temporary local disk data was not included, and that Azure did not allow exporting the sandboxed training VM directly due to resource policies. Which statement about ARM templates and VM data best explains this behavior?',
        'An ARM template captures the JSON definition of the resources needed to deploy a solution (VM size, network config, OS image reference, etc.) — it is not a data backup mechanism, so it would not capture live temporary disk contents. Resource policies, such as those in a Learn sandbox, can also block certain actions like template export on specific resources. Option D is false; templates define configuration, not raw disk data. Option C is incorrect and would make the feature unusably destructive; exporting a template does not delete the source VM. Option A invents a nonexistent dependency between Application Insights and template export capability.', 6, TRUE);

VALUES ('2342d13d-3289-46c1-864e-79e5c15de85f', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso wants to protect physical machines and Azure VMs (Windows and Linux) with a single backup service that supports application-consistent snapshots and offers geo-redundant storage options, without limiting how long backup data can be retained. Which service satisfies all of these requirements as described in the module?',
        'Azure Backup is a Backup-as-a-Service offering that protects physical machines and VMs regardless of location, supports application-consistent (VSS) snapshots, offers both locally redundant and geo-redundant storage, unlimited data transfer, and no limit on how long backup data can be retained — all stored in a Recovery Services vault. Option B describes Site Recovery, which is focused on replication and failover for disaster recovery/business continuity, not long-term backup retention. Options C and D describe manual, unsupported approaches that lack the built-in application-consistency, retention flexibility, and management that Azure Backup provides natively.', 7, TRUE);

VALUES ('de04a0aa-2b8b-420e-bd06-0b48069ad12c', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso runs a two-tier application: a web tier and a SQL tier, each with two VMs. They want to ensure that a single rack-level power or network switch failure cannot bring down both VMs of either tier simultaneously, but they are not yet ready to adopt availability zones. Which configuration best satisfies this requirement using only the concepts covered in this module?',
        'one for the web tier and one for the SQL tier — placing both VMs of each tier in their respective set so Azure spreads them across separate fault and update domains — Best practice is to separate distinct application tiers into their own availability sets, since Azure guarantees VMs within a set run across multiple physical servers, racks, storage units, and network switches — protecting each tier from single points of failure independently. Option B mixes tiers in one set, which does not follow the recommended tier-separation practice and can complicate fault isolation per tier. Option C (Auto-shutdown) only schedules VM power-off events for cost savings and has no relationship to fault-domain resiliency. Option D misapplies VM extensions, which handle post-deployment software configuration, not hardware failure detection or fault domain isolation.', 8, TRUE);

VALUES ('641608b7-c425-4e6c-a271-3ba3df00c90d', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'During a planned Azure platform maintenance window, Contoso''s operations team is concerned that all VMs in their availability set might reboot simultaneously, causing a full application outage. Based on how update domains work, is this a valid concern under normal circumstances?',
        'Azure restarts only one update domain at a time during planned maintenance, so with VMs spread across multiple update domains the application should remain available throughout — During planned maintenance, only one update domain is rebooted at a time, which is precisely the mechanism that protects applications from a full outage — as long as VMs are properly distributed across update domains (the default is five), some instances remain available while others are updated. Option B contradicts the documented staggered update-domain behavior. Option A is inaccurate; planned maintenance events specifically can cause reboots, just staggered ones. Option D incorrectly ties update domain behavior to guest OS type, when it is actually a platform-level mechanism independent of the guest operating system.', 9, TRUE);

VALUES ('4867d26a-84c8-4809-a0ce-bf59af6c3f15', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'An architect at Contoso is deciding between placing a fleet of stateless web-tier VMs behind a load balancer using an availability set versus using a Virtual Machine Scale Set with autoscale. The workload experiences highly variable daily traffic, and the team wants capacity to expand and contract automatically without manual intervention. Which option better satisfies this requirement, and why?',
        'Virtual Machine Scale Sets support autoscale, automatically increasing or decreasing the instance count based on defined rules — metric-based (e.g., CPU thresholds) or schedule-based — making them ideal for variable, unpredictable workloads. Option B is incorrect: an availability set is a static grouping construct for fault/update domain distribution and does not scale the VM count automatically by itself. Option C is wrong, since the two constructs serve fundamentally different purposes (fault-domain grouping versus elastic scaling). Option D is factually incorrect, since autoscale is a documented, supported capability of scale sets.', 10, TRUE);

VALUES ('2bb240f8-edc0-4c8e-ad77-fa20f5405ada', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso is creating a new Virtual Machine Scale Set and needs to decide on an orchestration mode. Their workload requires some instances to use a larger VM size for batch jobs while most instances use a smaller size for steady-state traffic, all within the same scale set. Which orchestration mode supports this mixed configuration, and what is Microsoft''s general guidance for new deployments?',
        'Flexible orchestration mode allows VMs within the same scale set to use different images, sizes, or configurations, and Microsoft recommends it as the default for most new deployments unless there''s a specific need for uniform, identical instances. Option B incorrectly attributes mixed-size support and default status to Uniform mode, which instead creates all instances from the same base image and configuration. Option C is false, since Flexible mode explicitly supports this scenario. Option D is an invented and unnecessary requirement; mixed sizes are handled within a single scale set, not via separate subscriptions.', 11, TRUE);

VALUES ('41ca120e-a9a5-4792-b789-883865ac826d', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso configured a Virtual Machine Scale Set using the "Fixed Spreading" algorithm, expecting VMs to always be spread across exactly five fault domains. During a scale-out event in a region with only three available fault domains, the deployment failed. What is the most likely explanation, and what alternative would Microsoft recommend to avoid this issue?',
        'With Fixed Spreading, VMs are always distributed across exactly five fault domains; if fewer than five are available in a given zone/region, the scale set deployment fails. Max Spreading instead distributes VMs across as many fault domains as are available, succeeding even with fewer than five — which is why Microsoft recommends it for most implementations. Option C is factually wrong and contradicts the documented behavior of Fixed Spreading. Option B misattributes the failure to autoscale, which is unrelated to fault domain spreading algorithms. Option A reverses the actual relationship — Max Spreading is the more flexible, not more demanding, option.', 12, TRUE);

VALUES ('19c79f80-82bf-4f9b-a8df-d7355e27a032', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s e-commerce site experiences a predictable traffic spike every Friday at 6 PM and a predictable lull every weekday at 3 AM. The team wants Azure to preemptively add capacity just before the Friday spike and reduce capacity during the early morning lull, rather than reacting only after CPU thresholds are crossed. Which autoscale rule type best fits this specific requirement?',
        'Time-based (schedule-based) rules let you scale ahead of anticipated load changes at specific, recurring dates and times — exactly matching Contoso''s predictable Friday-evening spike and weekday early-morning lull. Option A, purely metrics-based scaling, only reacts after the CPU threshold is crossed rather than proactively preparing capacity beforehand. Option B, availability zones, is a fault-tolerance/resiliency construct unrelated to scheduled capacity planning. Option C, update domains, governs how VMs are grouped for staggered maintenance reboots, not scaling schedules.', 13, TRUE);

VALUES ('7a6fb79f-b8c4-49f9-8187-59af1c642f64', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'An administrator at Contoso configured autoscale on a scale set with a minimum instance count of 2, a maximum of 10, but only defined a scale-out rule (no scale-in rule). After a traffic spike subsided over the weekend, the team noticed the instance count remained high, driving up costs unnecessarily. What is the most likely cause of this issue?',
        'A robust autoscale configuration always requires a combination of scale-out and scale-in rules. Without a scale-in rule, there is no logic instructing Azure to remove instances as load decreases, so the scale set remains at whatever elevated instance count the scale-out rule last produced — leading to unnecessary and high costs, exactly as the module warns. Option A is false; Azure does not auto-generate missing rules. Option D misunderstands the maximum instance count, which only caps the ceiling and has no bearing on reducing capacity. Option B describes a fabricated, nonexistent automatic monthly behavior.', 14, TRUE);

VALUES ('f16c3f0b-8479-4a0a-bb26-792e38807189', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso deploys VMs across three availability zones in a region for a mission-critical application and also wants protection against an entire regional outage (not just a datacenter-level failure). Which additional design consideration, mentioned in the module, should Contoso combine with availability zones to achieve full business continuity?',
        'The module specifically advises combining a mix of availability zones and Azure regional pairs to achieve full business continuity — zones protect against datacenter-level failures within a region, while regional pairs provide protection against a failure affecting an entire region. Option B overstates what zones alone can do; they do not protect against a region-wide outage. Option A is incorrect, since update domains are a maintenance-staggering mechanism within a single location, offering far less resiliency than zones or regional pairs. Option C is an irrelevant and incorrect claim; autoscale has no bearing on regional outage risk.', 15, TRUE);

VALUES ('b13300ed-dd57-4d29-98e3-7f872141674a', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s compliance team asks whether adding their application''s VMs to an availability set alone is sufficient to protect the application from failures caused by a bug in the application code itself. What is the accurate answer based on the module''s guidance?',
        'availability sets protect against underlying hardware/network failures and updates, but they do not protect against OS- or application-specific failures, which require separate disaster recovery and backup techniques — The module explicitly notes that adding VMs to an availability set does not protect applications from OS- or application-specific failures; other disaster recovery and backup techniques must be explored to provide that layer of protection. Option B fabricates a code-rollback capability that availability sets do not have. Option C overstates the guarantee; no infrastructure construct can guarantee 100% uptime against application-level bugs. Option D understates the real value of availability sets, which do meaningfully protect against hardware, rack, and update-related failures — just not application code defects.', 16, TRUE);

VALUES ('e4337d47-0d2b-4a75-9c2f-9f6abd1d3f14', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso hosts five low-traffic internal tools in a single Standard-tier App Service plan to save costs. One of the five tools, a reporting dashboard, suddenly experiences a huge spike in usage during month-end close and begins consuming most of the plan''s available resources, causing the other four tools to become slow or unresponsive. What is the recommended remediation based on the module''s guidance?',
        'The module specifically recommends isolating an application in a new App Service plan when it is resource-intensive or needs to scale independently of other apps in an existing plan — exactly the dashboard''s situation during month-end close. Option C is an unnecessarily destructive overreaction when isolation solves the problem without data loss. Option A would make the contention dramatically worse, since the Free tier offers no scale-out capability at all and is intended only for development/testing. Option B is incorrect; isolation into a separate plan is the documented, straightforward solution.', 17, TRUE);

VALUES ('bccc1baa-c198-4e50-b12a-d5ea5ac7f74c', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso is evaluating pricing tiers for a new production web application that requires 5 staging deployment slots and rule-based autoscaling, but does not need network-level isolation. Which pricing tier category should they select as the minimum that satisfies both requirements without paying for unneeded isolation features?',
        'The Standard tier is designed for production workloads, supports 5 staging slots and rule-based autoscale, and runs on dedicated (not shared or network-isolated) compute — matching Contoso''s stated needs precisely without paying for the network isolation only the Isolated tiers provide. Option C is incorrect, since Free-tier apps run on shared VMs with no scale-out capability and no deployment slots at all. Option B recommends unnecessary and costlier network isolation the requirement doesn''t call for. Option A is factually wrong; the Shared tier offers no scale-out/autoscale capability, unlike Standard.', 18, TRUE);

VALUES ('0e1c1618-d871-491c-98b0-b2ab4a514f46', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'A Contoso developer configured autoscale on their App Service plan with a minimum instance count of 3 and a maximum of 3, hoping this would let the app scale dynamically between quiet and busy periods. After deployment, the instance count never changed regardless of load. What misconfiguration explains this behavior?',
        'The module advises verifying that minimum and maximum instance count values are different, with adequate margin between them, since autoscale can only move within that range using the rules you define. Setting min = max = 3 leaves zero room to scale in either direction. Option A is false, since rule-based autoscale is available starting at the Standard tier, not just PremiumV3. Option B is incorrect, since App Service explicitly supports both metrics-based and time-based autoscale rules. Option C misunderstands the default instance count''s role, which only applies when metrics are unavailable, not as a permanent override.', 19, TRUE);

VALUES ('20f9a3b4-276f-4f0b-afe8-b9d3c85718d8', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s platform team wants an App Service scaling approach that requires the least ongoing rule configuration and responds immediately to unpredictable spikes in HTTP traffic, and they are willing to move to a higher pricing tier to get it. Which specific feature and tier combination satisfies this, according to the module?',
        'Automatic scaling (elastic scale) is explicitly recommended when a team wants less management overhead, cannot predict load patterns, or needs rapid response to traffic changes without configuring rules — and it is available only on PremiumV2 and PremiumV3. Option A is invalid because the Free tier has no scale-out or autoscale capability whatsoever. Option D is incorrect, since manual scaling requires an administrator to actively change instance counts and does not react automatically to anything. Option C misapplies deployment slots, which are for staged rollouts and swaps, not automatic traffic-based scaling.', 20, TRUE);

VALUES ('41b6c7a8-7e26-4f63-988a-79afb441f32f', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s monthly cost report shows their App Service plan billed at a flat rate regardless of how many of the three apps hosted in that plan were actively receiving traffic. A junior engineer assumes each app is billed separately based on its own usage. Which statement corrects this misunderstanding?',
        'You pay for the compute resources your App Service plan allocates, and all applications placed in that plan run on and share those same resources — which is precisely why placing multiple apps in one plan can create cost savings, and why the bill reflects the plan''s tier and instance count rather than per-app usage. Option B misrepresents the billing model; there''s no individual per-app metering in a shared plan. Option A invents a nonexistent "free app" allowance. Option D is factually wrong — plans are explicitly designed to host one or more applications simultaneously.', 21, TRUE);

VALUES ('1bc8605c-76f8-4445-814d-c4ff910132bd', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso needs to select an App Service pricing tier for a mission-critical financial application that must run on dedicated virtual machines inside a dedicated virtual network, isolated from all other customers'' network traffic, with the maximum available scale-out capacity. Which tier category satisfies every one of these requirements?',
        'Isolated and IsolatedV2 run dedicated Azure VMs on dedicated Azure virtual networks, providing network isolation in addition to compute isolation, and offer the maximum scale-out capabilities (up to 100 instances) of any tier — precisely matching the financial application''s strict isolation and scale requirements. Option B is the opposite of isolated: Free and Shared tiers run on shared VMs, potentially alongside other customers'' apps. Option C, Basic, provides dedicated compute but not the dedicated virtual network isolation required. Option D, Standard, also lacks network isolation and offers lower maximum scale-out than Isolated tiers.', 22, TRUE);

VALUES ('008b1c4c-c7cf-40d8-8982-6feedef2e639', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'An App Service plan at Contoso is configured with a scale-out rule that adds 2 instances when CPU exceeds 70%, but the team notices scaling reacts to brief, momentary CPU spikes that resolve within seconds, causing unnecessary instance churn. Which autoscale configuration element, described in the module, would most directly help smooth out this behavior?',
        'The module notes that you should carefully choose the correct statistics for your diagnostics metrics, such as Average, Minimum, Maximum, and Total, and that the duration setting determines how long the autoscale engine looks back to average metric usage, letting the metric stabilize before triggering an action — directly addressing momentary spike sensitivity. Option D removes CPU-based reactivity entirely rather than tuning it, which doesn''t fit a scenario still requiring load-based scaling. Option A, changing the maximum instance count, only caps the ceiling and does not affect trigger sensitivity. Option B is incorrect; notifications only inform administrators of scaling events and do not cause or prevent triggering.', 23, TRUE);

VALUES ('9af7bd14-3ee5-40a6-8fc4-59155d9be253', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso is comparing the Essential (Basic) and Premium tiers for an application that has outgrown Basic''s traffic-handling and now requires advanced autoscale (rule-based and elastic) plus more staging slots. According to the plan comparison table in the module, which best characterizes the difference between these two tiers for autoscale capability?',
        'According to the plan feature comparison, the Basic (B1) tier''s autoscale column shows "Manual" only, while Premium (P1V3) shows "Rules, Elastic" — meaning Premium adds both rule-based and elastic (automatic) scaling capability that Basic lacks entirely. Option C ignores this documented difference. Option B reverses the actual capabilities. Option A is incorrect since the table clearly shows autoscale support beginning well before the Isolated tier.', 24, TRUE);

VALUES ('94c2180a-b38b-4202-b2e9-b8c09ee8c3d2', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso deployed a new App Service web app and wants zero-downtime deployments for future updates, with the ability to validate changes before customers see them and a quick way to undo a bad release. Which App Service capability and workflow satisfies all three requirements together?',
        'Deployment slots let you validate changes in a staging slot before swapping into production, and swaps are seamless with all target-slot instances pre-warmed, eliminating downtime; if the swapped-in version has problems, performing the same swap again restores the previous "last known good" production app. Option A ignores the entire slots feature, forcing risky direct-to-production deployments. Option B is wrong because the Free tier doesn''t support deployment slots at all — they require Standard, Premium, or Isolated v2. Option D fabricates an automatic-swap capability that Application Insights (a monitoring tool) does not have.', 25, TRUE);

VALUES ('1f6aa8dc-7421-447b-8fbb-bbc502cee430', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'After swapping a staging slot into production, Contoso''s team is confused why the custom domain and TLS certificate binding did not move with the app, while the connection strings did. What explains this observed behavior?',
        'The module''s settings table explicitly lists custom domain names and non-public certificates/TLS settings as slot-specific items that remain with the originating slot, while connection strings (unless explicitly marked slot-specific) are among the settings swapped along with the app content. Option A contradicts the documented distinction between swapped and slot-specific settings. Option D incorrectly claims connection strings never swap, when by default they do. Option B is an unsupported assumption; the described behavior is expected and by design, not a failure.', 26, TRUE);

VALUES ('49ea693e-1432-44cf-a096-7d012e73dc85', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s compliance officer requires that unauthenticated users be completely blocked from reaching any part of a new internal HR application hosted on App Service, with no code changes to the app itself. Which App Service authentication configuration satisfies this requirement, and what caution should the team be aware of?',
        'but this is unsuitable for apps that need a public landing page, which is not a concern here for an internal HR app — Requiring authentication redirects all anonymous requests to \`/.auth/login/\<provider\>\` (or returns HTTP 401 for native mobile clients), blocking unauthenticated access to every part of the app without any code changes — appropriate here since an internal HR tool doesn''t need a public landing page. Option C is the literal opposite configuration; "Allow unauthenticated requests" explicitly permits anonymous traffic through to the app. Option A is false, since App Service''s built-in authentication module handles this without requiring custom code. Option D misattributes an authentication/authorization capability to Application Insights, which is a monitoring tool, not a security gatekeeper.', 27, TRUE);

VALUES ('c05ad9c2-c3a8-4af7-9c84-7e11c9d7e44d', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso wants their marketing site, currently at \`contoso-marketing-482.azurewebsites.net\`, to instead be reachable at \`www.contoso.com\`, and their DNS registrar does not allow CNAME records at the domain apex. What is the correct sequence of steps and record type to achieve this?',
        'The process requires reserving/validating the domain, creating a DNS record that maps the domain to the app, and then activating the custom domain in the Azure portal; since CNAME records mapping name-to-name are often disallowed at the root/apex by registrars, an A record (mapping name directly to IP address) must be used instead in that case. Option B is false — custom domains require explicit DNS record configuration to route traffic correctly. Option C incorrectly assumes Azure can override a third-party registrar''s DNS record restrictions, which it cannot. Option D misapplies an MX record, which is used for mail routing, not web traffic routing.', 28, TRUE);

VALUES ('46c7f52e-9142-49ff-9ea0-596212c73112', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s Basic-tier App Service application needs both a production and staging slot backup strategy, but the team discovers they can only configure backups for the production slot. What explains this limitation, and what would resolve it?',
        'Backup and restore is supported in Basic, Standard, Premium, and Isolated tiers, but the module specifically notes that at the Basic tier, you can only back up and restore the production slot — a tier upgrade is required for broader slot backup support. Option C is false, since several tiers do support this feature. Option A incorrectly claims a permanent limitation across all tiers, when it is specifically a Basic-tier constraint. Option B is wrong; the Free tier does not support backup and restore at all, making it a worse choice, not a solution.', 29, TRUE);

VALUES ('5e9d1f37-fa67-44f7-85a2-825e4e1e77ea', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso restored a full backup of their App Service app to recover from a bad deployment, but afterward noticed that a log file that had been manually uploaded to the site after the backup was taken is now missing. What is the most accurate explanation for this outcome?',
        'including files added after the backup was taken — is deleted — The module explicitly states that when you restore a full backup, all site content is replaced with everything in the backup, and if a file exists on the site but not in the backup, it is deleted — which is exactly what happened to the log file added after the backup snapshot was taken. Option D contradicts this documented replace-not-merge behavior. Option C invents a quarantine mechanism that does not exist for full restores. Option A is directly contradicted by the module''s explicit description of full restore behavior.', 30, TRUE);

VALUES ('018597d9-fd90-4b36-98ba-58e7729676a7', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s development team wants to understand which pages of their App Service web application are visited most, correlate slow page loads with specific exceptions, and track a custom business metric (number of items added to cart). Which single Azure feature, integrated with App Service, addresses all three of these needs?',
        'Application Insights, part of Azure Monitor, can track page views and load performance, correlate exceptions from server and browser with related requests, and record custom events and metrics such as items sold — directly addressing all three of Contoso''s needs in a single integrated feature. Option A, deployment slots, is for staged rollouts and has no monitoring or analytics capability. Option B, custom domains, only affects the app''s URL and has nothing to do with usage analytics. Option C, backup and restore, protects data and configuration but provides no insight into usage patterns or performance.', 31, TRUE);

VALUES ('c7a85e22-b609-4292-a1b3-18a1e029222c', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso connected their App Service app to a GitHub repository for continuous deployment. A developer wants to know which two build providers they can choose between for this GitHub integration, and which one is the default. What is the accurate answer?',
        'When connecting a GitHub repository to Azure App Service for continuous deployment, you can choose between GitHub Actions, which serves as the default build provider, and the App Service Build Service as an alternative. Option A incorrectly names Azure DevOps Pipelines and Jenkins, which are not the two GitHub-integration build provider options described. Option D is wrong, since GitHub integration explicitly supports automated CI/CD, not just manual FTP uploads. Option B incorrectly substitutes Bitbucket- and CircleCI-branded options that are not part of the documented GitHub build provider choice.', 32, TRUE);

VALUES ('2a0d16e1-0116-45d3-9324-ce4f1f7f7d5e', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s App Service application intermittently unloads and experiences cold-start delays during periods with no active user traffic, which is causing a scheduled CRON-triggered WebJob to fail silently. Which configuration setting most directly resolves this issue?',
        'Always On keeps the application loaded even when there is no incoming traffic, and the module specifically states this setting is required for continuous WebJobs or WebJobs triggered using a CRON expression — directly resolving the described failure. Option C, session affinity, only controls routing a specific client to the same instance during a session; it does not prevent the app from unloading. Option A is incorrect, since Free-tier apps are not exempt from idle unloading behavior. Option D is false; deployment slots follow the same Always On behavior as any other slot and are not inherently exempt from unloading.', 33, TRUE);

VALUES ('c6a3a5d1-f4c6-4534-b64d-ef816f012efc', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s application requires that all HTTP traffic be automatically redirected to HTTPS to comply with a new security policy, without requiring any change to the application''s own code. Which App Service setting accomplishes this?',
        'The module explicitly lists "HTTPS Only" as an app setting where, when enabled, all HTTP traffic is redirected to HTTPS — satisfying the compliance requirement with a simple configuration change and no code modification. Option B, session affinity, only relates to routing a client to the same instance and has no connection to HTTPS enforcement. Option C, creating a custom domain, changes the app''s address but does not by itself force HTTPS redirection. Option D, Always On, only controls whether the app stays loaded during idle periods and has nothing to do with HTTP-to-HTTPS redirection.', 34, TRUE);

VALUES ('810fe2da-c4d8-4218-8cd9-72e5c7062a4a', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso needs to run a short-lived batch job in a container that must start within seconds, requires no persistent cluster management, and needs a public IP with a DNS name so an external partner system can reach it directly. Which Azure container service, as described in the module, is the best fit?',
        'ACI offers the fastest and simplest way to run a container in Azure without managing VMs or adopting a higher-level orchestrator, provides fast startup times (seconds), and supports public IP connectivity with a fully qualified domain name — matching every requirement in the scenario. Option C introduces unnecessary VM management overhead that ACI is specifically designed to avoid. Option A, self-managed AKS, is a heavier orchestration platform meant for complex, longer-running microservice architectures, not a single short-lived batch job. Option B, App Service Isolated tier, is designed for web applications, not standalone container batch jobs with direct public IP/DNS requirements.', 35, TRUE);

VALUES ('63fa94f6-c3ec-4c7e-9cf3-3c3559a3f759', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso is deploying a container group with two containers: one serving a web application on port 80, and another running a companion process that also needs to listen on port 80 for internal diagnostics. During testing, they discover this configuration doesn''t work as expected. What is the underlying limitation, and what is the workaround?',
        'Containers within a container group share a port namespace and IP address, so port mapping between containers isn''t supported — two containers in the same group cannot both bind the same port. The correct workaround is to have the diagnostics process listen on a different port. Option D is false; there is no automatic conflict resolution for duplicate ports within a group. Option C is incorrect, since containers in a group share the group''s single public IP rather than each getting their own. Option A is an overcorrection; multi-container groups are fully supported, just with the shared-port-namespace constraint in mind.', 36, TRUE);

VALUES ('fd0f3bad-b161-4678-8b08-0f3e5c57136c', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso wants to run a monitoring sidecar container that periodically checks their main application container''s health and raises an alert if it stops responding, with both containers sharing the same lifecycle and local network. Which ACI deployment pattern, and which specific benefit described in the module, matches this use case?',
        'The module explicitly describes an application monitoring scenario as a benefit of multi-container groups: a monitoring container regularly sends requests to the application container to confirm it''s running and responding correctly, triggering an alert if it identifies possible issues — precisely matching Contoso''s requirement, with both containers sharing lifecycle, resources, and local network. Option A incorrectly claims ACI cannot group containers, contradicting the entire concept of container groups. Option B incorrectly claims ACI has no multi-container capability, which is factually wrong. Option D unnecessarily escalates to VM-based infrastructure when ACI natively supports this pattern.', 37, TRUE);

VALUES ('6634abbf-e4e8-47ae-8f9c-bee8b108bdb2', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso deployed a container group and later deleted it as part of an environment cleanup. When they tried to redeploy an identical group the next day, they were surprised to receive a different public IP address and FQDN than before. What explains this behavior?',
        'The module states that when a container group is deleted, its IP address and FQDN are released — meaning a newly created group, even with identical configuration, is not guaranteed to receive the same address or domain name back. Option A is incorrect and contradicts the documented release behavior. Option D is false; deleting and recreating container groups is a normal, supported operation, not one requiring a support ticket. Option B is an incorrect assumption; the observed behavior is expected, documented platform behavior, not an error.', 38, TRUE);

VALUES ('9a7ff88c-5fd5-4c54-8dac-1f363a02ff91', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s security team is reviewing whether to use containers or virtual machines to host two competing external clients'' workloads on the same underlying infrastructure, given very strict tenant isolation requirements. Based on the isolation characteristics described in the module, what is the most appropriate recommendation?',
        'The module explicitly notes that a VM offers full isolation from the host operating system and other VMs, which is useful when a strong security boundary is critical — explicitly citing the example of hosting applications from competing companies on the same server or cluster. Containers, by contrast, generally provide only lightweight isolation and do not constitute as strong a security boundary. Option B directly contradicts this documented guidance. Option C ignores the explicitly stated difference in isolation strength. Option D incorrectly claims ACI eliminates isolation concerns, when container-based isolation remains inherently lighter-weight than VM-based isolation regardless of which container service is used.', 39, TRUE);

VALUES ('23868408-144b-4883-8bb2-7102e8de2634', '64b1d23b-5dcd-477b-86e9-973b2d22907e', 'SINGLE_CHOICE', 'Contoso''s architecture team is choosing between Azure Container Apps (ACA) and Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) for a new set of cloud-native microservices. They want a serverless experience with minimal infrastructure management and built-in scale-to-zero for event-driven workloads, and they do not need direct access to native Kubernetes APIs. Which service better matches these requirements, and why?',
        'ACA is described as a serverless platform that simplifies deployment and management of containerized applications by abstracting infrastructure, supports scaling based on HTTP traffic and event-driven processing (including scale-to-zero via KEDA-based scalers), and explicitly does not provide direct access to native Kubernetes APIs — matching every stated requirement. Option C incorrectly labels AKS as serverless; AKS instead gives teams fine-grained control over a managed Kubernetes cluster, which requires more infrastructure awareness, not less. Option D ignores the meaningful, documented differences between the two services in management model and API access. Option A is factually wrong, since ACA explicitly supports event-driven scaling including scaling to zero.', 40, TRUE);
